\description{
Fit one logistic regression per feature of a 0/1 feature-by-sample table, such as a detection table, and test each named coefficient with a Wald z.

mzLib's \code{LogisticRegression.Fit} models logit P(outcome = 1) = X beta for every feature (row)
separately, by maximum likelihood, and tests each coefficient with a two-sided Wald z. It is R's
\code{glm(y ~ 0 + X, family = binomial)} per feature, which mzLib reproduces to 1e-8 relative in the
coefficients (1e-6 in standard errors and p-values).
}
\details{
The usual question in proteomics: \strong{is a feature's chance of being measured related to the
condition?} Write 1 where a protein was quantified and 0 where it was not; a coefficient far
from 0 says its missing values are not missing at random.

A feature is reported - never dropped - when it cannot be fitted. \code{"separated"} is the one to
read carefully: the design splits its 0s from its 1s (or every outcome is the same), so no finite
estimate exists. R's \code{glm()} prints a huge coefficient and a warning there; mzLib reports the
status and \code{NA}. Look at \code{events} and \code{observed} for those rows.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_logistic_model_fit}:

  - \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per (reported coefficient, feature) - one block of
    \code{feature_count} rows per coefficient you named, in that order, each in the responses file's
    feature order: \code{feature}; \code{coefficient}; \code{status}; \code{estimate}, the maximum-likelihood
    coefficient in log-odds per coefficient unit, so \code{exp(estimate)} is the \strong{odds ratio};
    \code{standard_error} (log-odds per coefficient unit), from the inverse Fisher information at the
    estimate; \code{z} (standard errors), the Wald statistic; \code{p_value}, a fraction (0 to 1),
    two-sided and \strong{not adjusted across features}; \code{deviance} (deviance, -2 log L), the
    feature's residual deviance; \code{iterations}, IRLS iterations used (0 when no fit was
    attempted); \code{observed}, samples with a 0 or 1; and \code{events}, observed samples whose outcome
    is 1. Statistics are \code{NA} where \code{status} is not \code{"fitted"}.
  - \code{responses_file} and \code{design_file}, as absolute paths; \code{feature_count} features and
    \code{sample_count} samples read; \code{sample_names}; \code{coefficient_names} (every design column) and
    \code{tested_coefficients}; \code{max_iterations} (iterations) and \code{tolerance} (relative change in
    deviance) as you passed them, \code{NA} when left to mzLib; \code{threads}; \code{missing_count} response
    cells (cells); \code{status_counts}, features per status; \code{row_count} rows; \code{column_names}.
  - \code{caveats}: what the numbers are and are not, with a count of unfitted features by status.

}
\section{What it is not}{


Wald tests are the least reliable of the likelihood tests near separation and in small samples;
no likelihood-ratio test is offered, and Firth's penalized likelihood (logistf), which gives
finite estimates for separated features, is not implemented. Send one coefficient's \code{p_value}
column to \code{\link{stats_adjust}} for Benjamini-Hochberg.

}
