\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item Maximum likelihood by iteratively reweighted least squares, started and stopped as R's glm does; standard errors from the inverse Fisher information at the estimate; Wald z tests. mzLib matches R 4.6.1's glm(family = binomial, epsilon = 1e-14) to 1e-8 relative in coefficients and deviance and to 1e-6 relative in standard errors and p-values (summary.glm takes its weights from the step before the estimate). Wald tests are the least reliable of the likelihood tests near separation and in small samples; no likelihood-ratio test is offered.
\item separated features carry no statistics. R's glm reports large, meaningless coefficients and a warning for them instead. A separated feature can be the strongest effect in the table (detected in every run of one condition and in none of another): read its events and observed counts. Firth's penalized likelihood (logistf), which gives finite estimates there, is not implemented.
\item p_value is not adjusted across features. Send one coefficient's p_value column to stats adjust for Benjamini-Hochberg.
\item A 0 is an outcome (for a detection table: measured as absent), not a missing value. Missing cells are omitted per feature, never imputed.
}
}
